\section{The spike study: design, results and localization}\label{sec:S7}

\subsection{The exploratory design}\label{sec:S7.1}

The detection study ran the noise model in the time domain, with each component scaled so that its in-band covariance equaled that of the same head's frequency-domain model and the room field not projected out (Table~\ref{tab:S18}). The spike was the spike-wave complex of \citet{hunold2016}, digitized, stretched in time by << F.g4_stretches >> (<< F.g4_n_morphologies >> morphologies) and passed through the band-pass filter of the data.

Spikes were placed at << F.g4_n_locations >> locations stratified by depth below the scalp (<< F.g4_band_10_20mm >>, << F.g4_band_20_30mm >>, << F.g4_band_30_45mm >> and << F.g4_band_45_70mm >> mm; << F.g4_locations_per_band >> locations per band) and by orientation (<< F.g4_n_orientation_bands >> bands from radial to tangential; << F.g4_locations_per_stratum >> locations per depth and orientation stratum). Each location received << F.g4_n_focal_events_per_location >> focal-dipole events, at << F.g4_n_strengths >> strengths of << F.g4_strengths_nam >> nAm, each with every morphology, and patch events of << F.g4_patch_radius_mm >> mm radius carrying the same total moments with the unstretched morphology. This gave << F.g4_n_focal_events >> focal and << F.g4_n_patch_events >> patch events, << F.g4_n_events_per_anatomy >> per anatomy, injected in random order and identically in every array. The adult's locations lay << F.g4_adult_locations_frontal >> in the frontal lobe, << F.g4_adult_locations_parietal >> in the parietal, << F.g4_adult_locations_temporal >> in the temporal, << F.g4_adult_locations_cingulate >> in the cingulate, << F.g4_adult_locations_insula >> in the insula and << F.g4_adult_locations_occipital >> in the occipital lobe. Their median depths were << F.g4_adult_median_depth_10_20mm >>, << F.g4_adult_median_depth_20_30mm >>, << F.g4_adult_median_depth_30_45mm >> and << F.g4_adult_median_depth_45_70mm >> mm in the four bands.

Every array, Neuromag and the dense and site-matched optically pumped magnetometer (OPM) arrays, was read by two detectors, and Neuromag also by its magnetometers and by its gradiometers alone (<< F.g4_n_detectors >> sensor sets per anatomy). Both detectors whitened the data with a Ledoit--Wolf covariance \citep{ledoit2004} estimated from null data and filtered them with the spike templates. The oracle detector tests the whitened, template-filtered projection on the true field pattern at the true peak (one-sided; per-trial false-positive probability << F.g4_oracle_alpha >>).

The practical detector takes at every sample the largest normalized output over the templates and the candidate field patterns of a cortical grid that excludes the true sources (Table~\ref{tab:S18}). Its local maxima at least << F.g4_refractory_s >> s apart are events. A spike counts as detected when an event above threshold lies within ±<< F.g4_hit_tolerance_ms >> ms of its peak; the same events count as false on null data. Like the oracle, it uses the simulated morphologies and the true forward model (main text, Section 2.5). Whether it is equally idealized for every array was not tested in the exploratory run. The pre-specified run's mismatch variant tests one departure (Section~\ref{sec:S7.5}), and focal on-scalp patterns may be more sensitive to forward-model and coregistration error.

Its thresholds for a nominal << F.g4_operating_point_per_minute >> false event per minute were set on independent null data and frozen. On held-out null data they gave << F.g4_heldout_rate_range >> false events per minute across sensor sets and anatomies (Table~\ref{tab:S9}). In the adult, the practical detector needed << F.g4_adult_practical_over_oracle_s50_10_20mm_range >> times the oracle's strength for half detection at << F.g4_band_10_20mm >> mm.

